  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are constrained by existing literature biases, including conceptual ambiguity, a heavy focus on planning rather than implementation, and a general lack of empirical evidence from LMIC contexts.",
      "what": "This systematic review analyzed 150 peer-reviewed studies published between 2003 and 2023 to identify the governance and contextual conditions shaping urban climate action. Following the PRISMA 2020 protocol, the study categorizes these conditions across vertical, horizontal, internal, and contextual domains, while distinguishing between mitigation and adaptation pathways in both high-income countries and low- and middle-income countries (LMICs).",
      "why": "It establishes distinct, configurational pathways for urban climate action, showing that mitigation is driven by norms and incentives, adaptation is driven by mandates and extreme weather risks, and LMIC contexts often rely on donor-led or co-benefit pathways due to centralized governance and limited vertical support."
    }
  },
